    "message": "feat(admin): discover Claude Code settings files and say which world each is in\n\nConfiguring the endpoint and token meant typing a path into a text box, with\none \"candidate\" listed whether or not anything was there. On a machine running\nWSL that is precisely the wrong affordance: there are two Claude Code\ninstallations and two ~/.claude/settings.json files, and \"my setting did not\napply\" is nearly always the other one.\n\nThe Connection section now discovers what actually exists and makes it a\nchoice:\n\n- Every settings.json this machine can reach, and only the ones that exist. A\n  path that could exist is noise on a list describing what is real.\n- Each labelled with the world it belongs to -- Windows, WSL (with the\n  distribution name), Linux, macOS -- as prominently as the path itself.\n- Each with its status against this server: configured for My Claude Code,\n  points somewhere else, not configured, or unreadable.\n- Pick one with a radio, press Configure to write the two keys. Remove appears\n  only when there is something to remove, and Configure reads \"Reconfigure\"\n  when the file already points here.\n- The editor below follows the same selection: selecting a file loads that\n  file's keys, so choosing where to connect and choosing what to edit are one\n  decision rather than two that can disagree. The editor names the file it is\n  editing.\n- When nothing is found, the section says so and offers to create the file at\n  the default location.\n\nDiscovery covers the native home including a CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR override, the\nWindows-side home when running under WSL, and every WSL distribution's home\nwhen running on Windows. The last one enumerates through `wsl.exe --list\n--quiet` with a timeout rather than listing \\wsl.localhost, because that UNC\nroot blocks for as long as the service takes to answer when no distribution is\nrunning -- and this runs while a page is loading. Every probe is wrapped:\na world that cannot be reached contributes nothing rather than failing the page.\n\nA project's .claude/settings.json still needs a typed path, since the server\ncannot know which repository you are in; that moved into a collapsed \"Use a\ndifferent file\" row rather than being the primary control.\n\nTwo bugs fixed:\n\n- The settings list vanished after Configure or Remove. Both handlers adopted\n  the write response as the new state, and that response carries only the\n  status of the file just written -- no targets. Both now re-read, which is\n  also what refreshes the other files' states after a write changes them.\n- The Remove button stayed on screen with `hidden` set, because `hidden` only\n  sets `display: none` as a UA default and `.secondary-button { display: flex }`\n  beat it. Added the standard `[hidden] { display: none !important }` guard,\n  which fixes this class of bug across the dashboard; verified view switching\n  and the review modal still work.\n\nVerification, real browser against a live server on a throwaway home (port\n8177), on a machine that genuinely has both worlds:\n- Discovery found the Windows file and the WSL Ubuntu-24.04 file, correctly\n  labelled, with the WSL one reported as pointing elsewhere.\n- Configure -> status flipped to \"Configured for My Claude Code\", Remove\n  appeared, button read \"Reconfigure\"; the file on disk gained exactly the two\n  keys. Remove -> both keys gone, status back to \"Not configured\", Remove\n  hidden again. The list survived both writes.\n- Selecting the WSL file re-pointed the editor at it and discovered 77\n  configured keys in it, grouped correctly. That file was only ever read: its\n  mtime is unchanged and no .fcc-backup sibling was created.\n- No console errors.\n- 18 new discovery tests, including a hanging wsl.exe, a missing wsl.exe, and\n  UTF-16 output. Two API contract tests rewritten for the new \"only what\n  exists\" behaviour, and pinned to the native world so they do not depend on\n  whether the developer's machine has WSL.\n- Full suite 3803 passed, same 14 environment-baseline failures as an\n  untouched worktree.\n\nMINOR bump 5.10.0 -> 5.11.0.",
